    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and it passes along with the full build.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the GOLD membership tier rule following the codebase's existing derived-field idiom (the same pattern used for `namesakeCount`): compute the value at create time, persist it on the `Owner`, and derive the tier from it in the mapper.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `householdSize` field (`household_size` column) with getter/setter, alongside `namesakeCount`.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 set `owner.setHouseholdSize(householdMembers.size() + 1)` at create time. `householdMembers` are the owners already sharing the same last name + address (which resolve to the same `householdId`); adding the new owner gives the household's size \"after this create\". This runs after the existing guard that rejects an unshared duplicate household, so the count is always accurate for the owner being created.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.membershipTier`** \u2014 GOLD now takes precedence: returns `GOLD` when `householdSize >= 3`, otherwise falls back to the existing SILVER (namesakeCount 0 + email present) and BRONZE rules.\n\n4. **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `household_size` column so the persisted field maps cleanly (tests use the `hsqldb` schema with `ddl-auto=none`).\n\n5. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `membershipTier` description to document the GOLD rule.\n\nThe new `coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold` acceptance test passes, and the full test suite builds green.",
